{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sh77fzkygLJBT696DRqVGR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\"command\":\"epic-review\",\"version\":1,\"status\":\"blocked\",\"outcome\":\"block\",\"result\":{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"canonicalReview\":{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"reviewerLabels\":[\"epic-reviewer-1\",\"epic-reviewer-2\"],\"reconciliationSummary\":\"The two reviewers disagree only on F-EPIC-001 (epic-reviewer-2's blocker); everything else is complementary. Independent re-verification sustains the blocker: against a clean default registry, `node dist/cli.js threads new-thread --file-path ''` returned `{ ok: true, value: { threadId: \\\"th_bd276835dfc0a9a2\\\", filePath: \\\"\\\" } }`, `threads resolve` returned that row, and `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_bd276835dfc0a9a2` failed with `{ errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\", code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\", reason: \\\"no thread file exists at \\\" }` — a registry row with no durable thread file, on the real SDK and built CLI. Three pieces of evidence harden it beyond reviewer-2's report: (1) the code's own comment at src/domains/threads/index.ts:66-69 calls the no-row-without-file invariant 'absolute', and the Story 1 boundary note in epic.md makes the same demand; (2) the CLI guard comment at src/cli/index.ts:99-100 explicitly names the empty-path/temp-database hazard, but the guard checks only `flags.filePath === undefined`, so an empty string slips past — the failure mode was known and the defense is incomplete; (3) the intake envelope's ThreadRefSchema (validate.ts:38) requires NonEmptyString for filePath while newThread accepts any string, an internal inconsistency on the same contract. No test covers an empty path (test/threads.test.ts has none). epic-reviewer-1's pass is explained by not probing this edge, not by contrary evidence, so per the conservative rule the blocker is not downgraded. All of epic-reviewer-1's non-blocking findings were independently re-verified in the current code and retained; reviewer-2's F-EPIC-002 and reviewer-1's NB-3 describe the same read-back ref-validation gap at the CLI and SDK layers respectively and are merged into one finding. Both reviewers' gate claims were re-run and confirmed: verify-all exits 0 (build, typecheck, lint OK over 42 files, boundaries OK over 38 files, 16 files / 111 tests passing).\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"The five stories compose into the designed pipeline: pure pre-lock validation → BEGIN IMMEDIATE → corruption check → per-event dedup/record/turn-transition/projection-with-membership-stamp/work-queueing → result assembly → COMMIT (src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts), with deterministic ids (m<eventOrder>, t<turnOrder>, w-<sourceId>-<kind>) consistent across stories and naturally idempotent for Epic 02.\",\"Story 1's thread-creation contract has a confirmed hole that every later story inherits: an empty file path registers a thread with no durable file, after which all thread-scoped operations on that id fail with thread_not_found. Later stories' substrate-building through Story 1's real surface is exactly why this single-surface defect is epic-wide (F-EPIC-001).\",\"Rigor drift across read surfaces: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema, but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations skip validateThreadRef, and the corresponding CLI commands skip the required-flag guard the intake commands have — fail-closed behavior is preserved but the error vocabulary differs (merged finding F-EPIC-002).\",\"Later stories' tests build state through earlier stories' real surfaces (thread creation via newThread, events via messageEvents), giving continuous upstream re-verification; no story mocks a neighbor.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"Module structure matches the tech-design layout (domains/{threads,intake-stream,messages,turns} with index.ts + internal/, tech-utils, shared, cli, sdk.ts); the boundary gate passes: 'boundaries: OK (38 files checked, fixtures exempt)', with test/fixtures/ as the sanctioned below-SDK exemption.\",\"Thread creation validates nothing about the path beyond existsSync (src/domains/threads/index.ts:73); node:sqlite's DatabaseSync('') opens a temporary database, so createThreadFile succeeds without producing a durable file, and the registry row lands anyway — violating the invariant the same file's comment calls absolute. The intake envelope already types filePath as NonEmptyString (validate.ts:38), so the fix aligns newThread with an existing contract decision rather than inventing one.\",\"Sound beyond-design addition: thread-file schema is a versioned migration history (v1 events → v4 work items) with lazy upgrade and identity validation before migration (threads/internal/create.ts), consistent with file-is-authority and well-tested.\",\"Benign interface drift: messages.createFromEvent takes a third turnId parameter versus the design's two-arg signature — the natural realization of the design's stamp-after-transition rule; turns.applyEvent matches the design exactly.\",\"Epic-vs-design contract drift: epic Flow 4 declares empty required payload fields invalid, but validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63) per the tech design's plain-string interfaces, so empty strings pass while empty actor/harness/idempotencyKey/toolCallId/toolName are rejected via NonEmptyString (NB-1, needs human ruling).\",\"Transaction boundaries, BEGIN IMMEDIATE, file-then-row creation with compensation, registry lazy-init with non-creating reads, tokenizer pinning (js-tiktoken o200k_base recorded in thread metadata), and the operation-context shape match tech-design decisions 2, 3, 6, and 8.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"All 33 TCs from the epic appear as greppably-tagged passing tests across Flows 1–5, including both named cross-chunk deferrals closed in their owing stories (TC-1.4 in intake.test.ts; the TC-3.3/TC-3.6 work halves in work-queue.test.ts) and all eight architecture-risk rows from the test plan.\",\"Confirmed coverage gap behind the blocker: no test exercises an empty file path on threads.newThread or `threads new-thread --file-path ''`, so AC-1.1/AC-1.3/AC-1.4 are proven only for non-degenerate paths; test/threads.test.ts has no such case.\",\"Test quality is substantive: durability asserted through raw SQL row scans, rejection asserted through full five-surface logical read-back diffs against baselines, the 300KB tool-result round-trip runs through both SDK and spawned-binary stdin.\",\"111 tests across 16 files versus the plan's ~72 estimate; the overage is migration tests, per-story CLI-process splits, and supplemental strictness cases, all in-scope.\",\"Both gates re-verified by this canonical pass: verify-all (epic gate) exits 0 end to end including the spawned-binary CLI suite; green-verify's Red-manifest immutability check was reported passing by both reviewers.\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No fake adapters, mocks, or stubbed success on the production path: storage is real node:sqlite (WAL, foreign keys, busy_timeout), token counting is real js-tiktoken o200k_base, and every CLI command body is one SDK call plus JSON rendering.\",\"Material production-path defect (the blocker): the real SDK and built CLI create a registry row for an empty path with no thread file; reproduced this pass as `{ ok: true, value: { threadId: \\\"th_bd276835dfc0a9a2\\\", filePath: \\\"\\\" } }` followed by thread_not_found on read-back. The CLI's own guard comment names this exact hazard but only catches a missing flag, not an empty value (src/cli/index.ts:99-103).\",\"Two module-level test seams live in the production pipeline module (setIntakeWalkHook, setIntakeClock — pipeline.ts:23, 34): null/no-op in production, settable only through the boundary-exempt fixtures directory, fail-safe when unset; the walk hook is sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row, the clock setter is an addition. Neither fakes success (NB-4).\",\"Dead code and stale artifacts: shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no remaining callers; dist/ retains pre-epic compiled output (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) not produced by current src/ — the package is private so nothing ships, but tsc does not clean (NB-5).\",\"newThread stamps createdAt from the raw wall clock with no injection seam (threads/index.ts:85), a small departure from the test plan's claim that the injected clock covers createdAt; production behavior is correct.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-001\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Thread creation accepts an empty path and registers a thread with no durable file\",\"evidence\":\"src/domains/threads/index.ts:73 guards only with existsSync(input.filePath); node:sqlite's DatabaseSync('') opens a temporary database that vanishes on close, so newThread({ filePath: '' }) succeeds and inserts a registry row for filePath ''. Independently reproduced on the built CLI against a clean default registry: `threads new-thread --file-path ''` → { ok: true, value: { threadId: 'th_bd276835dfc0a9a2', filePath: '' } }; `threads resolve` returned the row; `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_bd276835dfc0a9a2` → { errorClass: 'caller_error', code: 'thread_not_found', reason: 'no thread file exists at ' }. This violates the no-row-without-file invariant the code itself documents as 'absolute' (threads/index.ts:66-69) and the Story 1 boundary note in epic.md, and breaks AC-1.1 (no file created), AC-1.3 (no file to read the id from), and AC-1.4 (row points at nothing). Corroborating evidence the hazard was known: the CLI guard comment at src/cli/index.ts:99-100 names the empty-path/temp-database failure mode, but the guard checks only `flags.filePath === undefined`, so an empty string passes; and the intake envelope's ThreadRefSchema already requires NonEmptyString for filePath (validate.ts:38) while newThread accepts any string. No test covers the empty-path case on SDK or CLI. Fix is small and contract-aligned: reject empty (or whitespace-only) filePath in newThread with a caller_error before any storage touch, plus SDK and CLI tests.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.1\",\"AC-1.3\",\"AC-1.4\",\"TC-1.1\",\"TC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":true}],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"NB-1\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Empty-string payload text/content accepted despite epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule\",\"evidence\":\"Epic Flow 4 lists 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' as invalid. validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63), so { text: '' } and empty tool_result content pass validation, while empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey are rejected via NonEmptyString. The tech design's interfaces typed these as plain string, so the drift originates between epic and design; the implementation followed the design. Whether an empty tool result (a real occurrence) should be a rejection is a product call.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/validate.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.2\",\"AC-4.4\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-2\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"CLI parses flags with strict:false, silently ignoring unknown or misspelled flags\",\"evidence\":\"src/cli/index.ts:53-64 calls parseArgs with strict: false, so `lhc threads new-thread --file-path p --titel x` creates the thread with no title and no error. This is the silent-drop failure mode the epic's boundary philosophy ('Nothing is silently dropped') exists to prevent, though the closed-contract rule formally covers the event envelope, not argv. A strict: true parse with a structured usage error would align the CLI boundary with the SDK boundary's strictness.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-002\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Read-back surfaces skip thread-reference validation at both SDK and CLI layers\",\"evidence\":\"Merged from epic-reviewer-1 NB-3 and epic-reviewer-2 F-EPIC-002 — the same gap at two layers. SDK: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92, 267), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations pass the reference straight to resolveThreadRef, so unknown fields or an empty filePath are tolerated. CLI: `messages list`, `messages list-queued-work`, `turns list`, and `turns list-queued-work` (cli/index.ts:176-183) lack the --thread-id/--file-path missing-flag guard the intake commands have, and threadRefFrom defaults a missing path to '' (cli/index.ts:79); verified this pass that `node dist/cli.js messages list` returns thread_not_found for an empty path instead of a usage error. Behavior remains fail-closed; the inconsistency is in which error the caller gets and whether unknown ref fields are named.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.6\",\"AC-4.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-4\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Mutable test seams exported from the production pipeline module\",\"evidence\":\"setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:23, 34) are module-global mutable state in production code, reachable by any importer of the internal module path even though the boundary check confines them to test/fixtures and the package export map exposes only the SDK root. Unset they are inert (real wall clock when unset; explicit clock argument wins). The walk hook is design-sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row; the clock setter is an addition beyond the design's context-injected clock. Consider relocating behind a single test-only entry or documenting them in the design's seam inventory when Epic 02 extends the context.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/intake-seam.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-5\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Dead stub helper and stale dist artifacts left from earlier phases\",\"evidence\":\"shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no callers since the Story 0 CLI stubs were replaced (verified: only the definition remains in src/). dist/ retains compiled output from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/ confirmed present) because tsc does not clean; the package is private so nothing ships, but a clean step would prevent confusion about what the production build contains.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts\",\"packages/lhc/dist\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[\"Whether epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule applies to payload text/content fields (NB-1): the epic prose says reject, the tech design's typed interfaces say accept, and the implementation follows the design. Needs a human ruling and an erratum on whichever document loses.\",\"The work_item table has no UNIQUE/status constraints beyond the primary key by design (Epic 02 adds lifecycle); confirm Epic 02's schema migration owns adding the status vocabulary constraint so 'queued'-only is not silently relied on.\"],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"},\"verifierResults\":[{\"resultId\":\"121ad125-b9d9-4f3e-b3d0-1ea2dc538681\",\"outcome\":\"pass\",\"provider\":\"claude-code\",\"model\":\"claude-fable-5\",\"reviewerLabel\":\"epic-reviewer-1\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"The five stories compose into one coherent pipeline exactly as designed: validate (pure, pre-lock) → BEGIN IMMEDIATE → corruption check → per-event [dedup → record → turn transition → projection with membership stamp → work queueing] → walk-time result assembly → COMMIT (src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts:84-254). The transition-before-stamp ordering is implemented and TC-3.8's two-turns-in-one-batch case works without lookahead.\",\"Deterministic id scheme is consistent across stories: m<eventOrder> (messages/index.ts:63), t<turnOrder> (turns/internal/store.ts:27), w-<sourceId>-<kind> (tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:40), matching tech-design decision 7; work-item ids are naturally idempotent for the Epic 02 repair path.\",\"The error model (OpResult, three classes, stable codes) is uniform across all four domain surfaces and the CLI; each class has exactly one producer (validation → caller_error, corruption check → state_corruption, wrapped SQLite/fs → system_error) as the design requires, and TC-4.4/turns tests assert all three classes against each other through both SDK and CLI.\",\"Later stories' tests build their substrate through earlier stories' real surfaces (threadWith-style helpers create threads via newThread and record via messageEvents), giving the continuous upstream re-verification the design called for; no story mocks a neighbor.\",\"Minor inconsistency: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92-93, 267-268), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations skip validateThreadRef entirely — an unknown field on a read-back thread reference is silently tolerated there. The epic's closed-contract rule formally binds only the intake envelope, so this is drift in rigor, not a contract violation.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"Module structure matches the tech design's layout exactly (domains/{threads,intake-stream,messages,turns} with index.ts + internal/, tech-utils/{work-queue,token-counting}, shared/{context,errors,storage}, cli/{index,render}, sdk.ts); the boundary check (scripts/check-boundaries.mjs) enforces both rules with the fixtures exemption and passes over 38 files.\",\"Import boundaries hold: all cross-domain calls go through domain index surfaces; the only below-SDK reach is test/fixtures/ (corrupt.ts, intake-seam.ts, legacy.ts), which is the design's sanctioned exemption.\",\"Beyond-design addition (sound): the thread-file schema is a versioned migration history (v1 events, v2 messages, v3 turns, v4 work items) with lazy upgrade through openThreadDatabase and identity validation before any migration (threads/internal/create.ts:28-190). This is not in the tech design but is consistent with file-is-authority, is well-tested (F-03-001/F-03-002, thread-migration.test.ts), and protects pre-story files.\",\"Interface drift (benign): messages.createFromEvent takes a third turnId parameter (messages/index.ts:55-59) versus the design's two-arg signature — the natural realization of the design's own stamp-after-transition rule; turns.applyEvent matches the design signature exactly.\",\"Contract drift against the epic: Flow 4 declares 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' invalid, but validate.ts accepts empty-string text (user_prompt/assistant_text/assistant_thinking/runtime_note) and empty tool_result.content (validate.ts:55-65 uses Schema.String, not NonEmptyString), while rejecting empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey. The tech design's interfaces typed these as plain string, so the drift originates between epic and design. See non-blocking finding NB-1.\",\"Transaction boundaries, BEGIN IMMEDIATE, file-then-row creation with compensation, registry lazy-init with non-creating reads, tokenizer pinning (js-tiktoken:o200k_base recorded in thread metadata at creation), and the operation-context shape all match the design decisions 2, 3, 6, and 8 as written.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"All 33 TCs from the epic appear as greppably-tagged tests and pass: Flow 1 (TC-1.1–1.6 with CLI legs), Flow 2 (TC-2.1–2.9), Flow 3 (TC-3.1–3.8 plus the golden state-machine suite covering every rule-table row), Flow 4 (TC-4.1–4.5 plus the unknown-field and empty actor/harness supplementals), Flow 5 (TC-5.1–5.5 with the below-SDK rawDump absence scan for TC-5.5).\",\"Both named cross-chunk deferrals are explicitly closed in their owing stories: TC-1.4 id/path equivalence lives in intake.test.ts titled 'closes Story 1's deferral', and the TC-3.3/TC-3.6 work halves are paid in work-queue.test.ts titled 'Story 4's debt paid'.\",\"All eight architecture-risk rows from the test plan exist and pass: mid-walk rollback via the induced-failure seam, restart survival extended to work items, rejected-batch-takes-no-lock, system_error rollback parity, fixture validity, boundary-check coverage, registry lazy-init non-creation, plus migration coverage beyond the plan.\",\"Test quality is substantive, not title-deep: durability asserted through raw SQL row scans (work-queue.test.ts:64-72, 300-310), rejection asserted through full five-surface logical read-back diffs against baselines, and the 300KB tool-result round-trip runs through both SDK and spawned-binary stdin.\",\"111 tests across 16 files versus the plan's ~72 estimate — the overage is migration tests, CLI-process splits per story, and supplemental strictness cases, all in-scope.\",\"Gates: verify-all (epic gate) passes end to end including the spawned-binary CLI process suite; green-verify (story gate) passes including the Red-manifest test-immutability check (33+ red-phase files unchanged).\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No fake adapters, mocks, or stubbed success exist anywhere on the production path: storage is real node:sqlite (WAL, foreign keys, busy_timeout — shared/storage.ts:8-15), token counting is real js-tiktoken o200k_base, and every CLI command body is one SDK call plus JSON rendering. The test suite's what-gets-mocked-nothing claim holds in the code.\",\"Two module-level test seams live in the production pipeline module: setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:21-36). Both are null/no-op in production, settable only through the boundary-exempt fixtures directory, and fail safe (real wall clock when unset; explicit clock argument wins). The walk hook is design-sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row; the clock setter is an implementation addition beyond the design's context-injected clock. Reported as material per the audit mandate; neither fakes success or alters production behavior.\",\"Dead code: shared/errors.ts:36-45 notImplemented() is the Story 0 fail-closed stub vocabulary with no remaining callers.\",\"Stale build artifacts in dist/ from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) are not produced by the current src/ and would ship under files:[\\\"dist\\\"] if the package were ever published (it is private). The bin target dist/cli.js is current and rebuilt by every gate run.\",\"newThread stamps createdAt from the raw wall clock with no injection seam (threads/index.ts:85), a small departure from the test plan's claim that the injected clock covers createdAt; no test needed it and production behavior is correct.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"NB-1\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Empty-string payload text/content accepted despite epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule\",\"evidence\":\"Epic Flow 4 lists 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' as invalid. validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63), so { text: \\\"\\\" } and empty tool_result content pass validation, while empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey are rejected via NonEmptyString. The tech design's interface section typed these as plain string, so design and epic disagree; the implementation followed the design. Whether an empty tool result (a real occurrence — tools legitimately return empty output) should be a rejection is a product call.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/validate.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.2\",\"AC-4.4\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-2\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"CLI parses flags with strict:false, silently ignoring unknown or misspelled flags\",\"evidence\":\"cli/index.ts:53-64 calls parseArgs with strict: false, so `lhc threads new-thread --file-path p --titel x` creates the thread with no title and no error. This is the silent-drop failure mode the epic's boundary philosophy ('Nothing is silently dropped') exists to prevent, though the closed-contract rule formally covers the event envelope, not argv. A strict: true parse with a structured usage error would align the CLI boundary with the SDK boundary's strictness.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-3\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Read-back surfaces skip closed thread-reference validation\",\"evidence\":\"messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92, 267), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations pass the reference straight to resolveThreadRef, so an unknown field or empty filePath on a read-back call is tolerated (empty path surfaces as thread_not_found rather than invalid_event). Behavior is still fail-closed; the inconsistency is in which error the caller gets and whether unknown ref fields are named.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-4\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Mutable test seams exported from the production pipeline module\",\"evidence\":\"setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:21-36) are module-global mutable state in production code, reachable by any importer of the internal module path even though the in-repo boundary check confines them to test/fixtures and the package export map exposes only the SDK root. Unset they are inert. Consider relocating behind a single test-only entry or documenting them in the design's seam inventory when Epic 02 extends the context.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/intake-seam.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-5\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Dead stub helper and stale dist artifacts left from earlier phases\",\"evidence\":\"shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no callers since the Story 0 CLI stubs were replaced. dist/ retains compiled output from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) because tsc does not clean; the package is private so nothing ships, but a clean step or removing the strays would prevent confusion about what the production build contains.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts\",\"packages/lhc/dist\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[\"Whether epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule applies to payload text/content fields (NB-1): the epic prose says reject, the tech design's typed interfaces say accept, and the implementation follows the design. Needs a human ruling and an erratum on whichever document loses.\",\"The work_item table has no UNIQUE/status constraints beyond the primary key by design (Epic 02 adds lifecycle); confirm Epic 02's schema migration owns adding the status vocabulary constraint so 'queued'-only is not silently relied on.\"],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"},{\"resultId\":\"a5f0f865-007a-4fe3-9cb4-04c3b2214706\",\"outcome\":\"block\",\"provider\":\"codex\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.5\",\"reviewerLabel\":\"epic-reviewer-2\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"Stories 1-5 are wired through the same SDK/CLI production path and both required gates pass, but Story 1's thread-creation contract has an untested edge that breaks every later story by registering a thread with no durable file.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"The domain/import structure matches the tech-design layout and the boundary gate passed: `boundaries: OK (38 files checked, fixtures exempt)`.\",\"Thread creation does not validate non-empty paths at the SDK boundary; `newThread({ filePath: \\\"\\\" })` reaches `node:sqlite`, which opens a temporary database, then inserts a registry row for `filePath: \\\"\\\"`.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"The implemented tests cover the epic TC table broadly: `verify-all` reported 16 files and 111 tests passing.\",\"Coverage is missing for invalid empty thread file paths on `threads.newThread` and the CLI `threads new-thread --file-path ''` path, so AC-1.1/AC-1.3/AC-1.4 are not fully proven.\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No material fake adapter, shim, or placeholder execution path was found in the accepted intake, projection, turn, or work-queue flow; the stale `notImplemented` helper is present but unused by production commands.\",\"Material production-path defect: the real SDK and real built CLI can create a registry row for an empty path without creating a thread file. Exact observed CLI sequence: `threads new-thread --file-path ''` returned `{ ok: true, value: { threadId: \\\"th_0fea4c8735507259\\\", filePath: \\\"\\\" } }`; `threads resolve` returned that row; `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_0fea4c8735507259` then returned `{ ok: false, error: { errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\", code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\", reason: \\\"no thread file exists at \\\" } }`.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-001\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Thread creation accepts an empty path and registers a non-existent thread file\",\"evidence\":\"`packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts:73` only checks `existsSync(input.filePath)` before `createThreadFile`. Node's `DatabaseSync(\\\"\\\")` succeeds as a temporary database, so `newThread({ filePath: \\\"\\\" })` returned `ok: true` with `filePath: \\\"\\\"`, no file existed at that path, and the registry still resolved the generated thread id to `\\\"\\\"`. The built CLI reproduced the same behavior with `threads new-thread --file-path ''`, after which `intake-stream list-events --thread-id <id>` failed with `thread_not_found`.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.1\",\"AC-1.3\",\"AC-1.4\",\"TC-1.1\",\"TC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":true}],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-002\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Some read-back CLI commands do not enforce the required thread reference flags\",\"evidence\":\"`packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts:176` through `packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts:183` call `messages.list`, `messages.list-queued-work`, `turns.list`, and `turns list-queued-work` without the missing `--thread-id`/`--file-path` guard used by intake commands. Running `node packages/lhc/dist/cli.js messages list` returned `thread_not_found` for an empty path instead of a usage/missing-flag error.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/turns.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"CLI conventions\",\"AC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"}]},\"errors\":[],\"warnings\":[],\"artifacts\":[{\"kind\":\"result-envelope\",\"path\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/artifacts/epic/001-epic-review.json\"}],\"startedAt\":\"2026-06-10T15:12:46.467Z\",\"finishedAt\":\"2026-06-10T15:25:39.208Z\"}\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"10fd9328-dcd9-4bde-8fdf-f73f0364e72b","uuid":"09d2c2ce-738c-4539-a6c4-169c060564be","timestamp":"2026-06-10T15:49:06.234Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/artifacts/epic/001-epic-review.json","content":"{\"command\":\"epic-review\",\"version\":1,\"status\":\"blocked\",\"outcome\":\"block\",\"result\":{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"canonicalReview\":{\"outcome\":\"block\",\"reviewerLabels\":[\"epic-reviewer-1\",\"epic-reviewer-2\"],\"reconciliationSummary\":\"The two reviewers disagree only on F-EPIC-001 (epic-reviewer-2's blocker); everything else is complementary. Independent re-verification sustains the blocker: against a clean default registry, `node dist/cli.js threads new-thread --file-path ''` returned `{ ok: true, value: { threadId: \\\"th_bd276835dfc0a9a2\\\", filePath: \\\"\\\" } }`, `threads resolve` returned that row, and `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_bd276835dfc0a9a2` failed with `{ errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\", code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\", reason: \\\"no thread file exists at \\\" }` — a registry row with no durable thread file, on the real SDK and built CLI. Three pieces of evidence harden it beyond reviewer-2's report: (1) the code's own comment at src/domains/threads/index.ts:66-69 calls the no-row-without-file invariant 'absolute', and the Story 1 boundary note in epic.md makes the same demand; (2) the CLI guard comment at src/cli/index.ts:99-100 explicitly names the empty-path/temp-database hazard, but the guard checks only `flags.filePath === undefined`, so an empty string slips past — the failure mode was known and the defense is incomplete; (3) the intake envelope's ThreadRefSchema (validate.ts:38) requires NonEmptyString for filePath while newThread accepts any string, an internal inconsistency on the same contract. No test covers an empty path (test/threads.test.ts has none). epic-reviewer-1's pass is explained by not probing this edge, not by contrary evidence, so per the conservative rule the blocker is not downgraded. All of epic-reviewer-1's non-blocking findings were independently re-verified in the current code and retained; reviewer-2's F-EPIC-002 and reviewer-1's NB-3 describe the same read-back ref-validation gap at the CLI and SDK layers respectively and are merged into one finding. Both reviewers' gate claims were re-run and confirmed: verify-all exits 0 (build, typecheck, lint OK over 42 files, boundaries OK over 38 files, 16 files / 111 tests passing).\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"The five stories compose into the designed pipeline: pure pre-lock validation → BEGIN IMMEDIATE → corruption check → per-event dedup/record/turn-transition/projection-with-membership-stamp/work-queueing → result assembly → COMMIT (src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts), with deterministic ids (m<eventOrder>, t<turnOrder>, w-<sourceId>-<kind>) consistent across stories and naturally idempotent for Epic 02.\",\"Story 1's thread-creation contract has a confirmed hole that every later story inherits: an empty file path registers a thread with no durable file, after which all thread-scoped operations on that id fail with thread_not_found. Later stories' substrate-building through Story 1's real surface is exactly why this single-surface defect is epic-wide (F-EPIC-001).\",\"Rigor drift across read surfaces: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema, but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations skip validateThreadRef, and the corresponding CLI commands skip the required-flag guard the intake commands have — fail-closed behavior is preserved but the error vocabulary differs (merged finding F-EPIC-002).\",\"Later stories' tests build state through earlier stories' real surfaces (thread creation via newThread, events via messageEvents), giving continuous upstream re-verification; no story mocks a neighbor.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"Module structure matches the tech-design layout (domains/{threads,intake-stream,messages,turns} with index.ts + internal/, tech-utils, shared, cli, sdk.ts); the boundary gate passes: 'boundaries: OK (38 files checked, fixtures exempt)', with test/fixtures/ as the sanctioned below-SDK exemption.\",\"Thread creation validates nothing about the path beyond existsSync (src/domains/threads/index.ts:73); node:sqlite's DatabaseSync('') opens a temporary database, so createThreadFile succeeds without producing a durable file, and the registry row lands anyway — violating the invariant the same file's comment calls absolute. The intake envelope already types filePath as NonEmptyString (validate.ts:38), so the fix aligns newThread with an existing contract decision rather than inventing one.\",\"Sound beyond-design addition: thread-file schema is a versioned migration history (v1 events → v4 work items) with lazy upgrade and identity validation before migration (threads/internal/create.ts), consistent with file-is-authority and well-tested.\",\"Benign interface drift: messages.createFromEvent takes a third turnId parameter versus the design's two-arg signature — the natural realization of the design's stamp-after-transition rule; turns.applyEvent matches the design exactly.\",\"Epic-vs-design contract drift: epic Flow 4 declares empty required payload fields invalid, but validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63) per the tech design's plain-string interfaces, so empty strings pass while empty actor/harness/idempotencyKey/toolCallId/toolName are rejected via NonEmptyString (NB-1, needs human ruling).\",\"Transaction boundaries, BEGIN IMMEDIATE, file-then-row creation with compensation, registry lazy-init with non-creating reads, tokenizer pinning (js-tiktoken o200k_base recorded in thread metadata), and the operation-context shape match tech-design decisions 2, 3, 6, and 8.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"All 33 TCs from the epic appear as greppably-tagged passing tests across Flows 1–5, including both named cross-chunk deferrals closed in their owing stories (TC-1.4 in intake.test.ts; the TC-3.3/TC-3.6 work halves in work-queue.test.ts) and all eight architecture-risk rows from the test plan.\",\"Confirmed coverage gap behind the blocker: no test exercises an empty file path on threads.newThread or `threads new-thread --file-path ''`, so AC-1.1/AC-1.3/AC-1.4 are proven only for non-degenerate paths; test/threads.test.ts has no such case.\",\"Test quality is substantive: durability asserted through raw SQL row scans, rejection asserted through full five-surface logical read-back diffs against baselines, the 300KB tool-result round-trip runs through both SDK and spawned-binary stdin.\",\"111 tests across 16 files versus the plan's ~72 estimate; the overage is migration tests, per-story CLI-process splits, and supplemental strictness cases, all in-scope.\",\"Both gates re-verified by this canonical pass: verify-all (epic gate) exits 0 end to end including the spawned-binary CLI suite; green-verify's Red-manifest immutability check was reported passing by both reviewers.\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No fake adapters, mocks, or stubbed success on the production path: storage is real node:sqlite (WAL, foreign keys, busy_timeout), token counting is real js-tiktoken o200k_base, and every CLI command body is one SDK call plus JSON rendering.\",\"Material production-path defect (the blocker): the real SDK and built CLI create a registry row for an empty path with no thread file; reproduced this pass as `{ ok: true, value: { threadId: \\\"th_bd276835dfc0a9a2\\\", filePath: \\\"\\\" } }` followed by thread_not_found on read-back. The CLI's own guard comment names this exact hazard but only catches a missing flag, not an empty value (src/cli/index.ts:99-103).\",\"Two module-level test seams live in the production pipeline module (setIntakeWalkHook, setIntakeClock — pipeline.ts:23, 34): null/no-op in production, settable only through the boundary-exempt fixtures directory, fail-safe when unset; the walk hook is sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row, the clock setter is an addition. Neither fakes success (NB-4).\",\"Dead code and stale artifacts: shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no remaining callers; dist/ retains pre-epic compiled output (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) not produced by current src/ — the package is private so nothing ships, but tsc does not clean (NB-5).\",\"newThread stamps createdAt from the raw wall clock with no injection seam (threads/index.ts:85), a small departure from the test plan's claim that the injected clock covers createdAt; production behavior is correct.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-001\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Thread creation accepts an empty path and registers a thread with no durable file\",\"evidence\":\"src/domains/threads/index.ts:73 guards only with existsSync(input.filePath); node:sqlite's DatabaseSync('') opens a temporary database that vanishes on close, so newThread({ filePath: '' }) succeeds and inserts a registry row for filePath ''. Independently reproduced on the built CLI against a clean default registry: `threads new-thread --file-path ''` → { ok: true, value: { threadId: 'th_bd276835dfc0a9a2', filePath: '' } }; `threads resolve` returned the row; `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_bd276835dfc0a9a2` → { errorClass: 'caller_error', code: 'thread_not_found', reason: 'no thread file exists at ' }. This violates the no-row-without-file invariant the code itself documents as 'absolute' (threads/index.ts:66-69) and the Story 1 boundary note in epic.md, and breaks AC-1.1 (no file created), AC-1.3 (no file to read the id from), and AC-1.4 (row points at nothing). Corroborating evidence the hazard was known: the CLI guard comment at src/cli/index.ts:99-100 names the empty-path/temp-database failure mode, but the guard checks only `flags.filePath === undefined`, so an empty string passes; and the intake envelope's ThreadRefSchema already requires NonEmptyString for filePath (validate.ts:38) while newThread accepts any string. No test covers the empty-path case on SDK or CLI. Fix is small and contract-aligned: reject empty (or whitespace-only) filePath in newThread with a caller_error before any storage touch, plus SDK and CLI tests.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.1\",\"AC-1.3\",\"AC-1.4\",\"TC-1.1\",\"TC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":true}],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"NB-1\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Empty-string payload text/content accepted despite epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule\",\"evidence\":\"Epic Flow 4 lists 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' as invalid. validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63), so { text: '' } and empty tool_result content pass validation, while empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey are rejected via NonEmptyString. The tech design's interfaces typed these as plain string, so the drift originates between epic and design; the implementation followed the design. Whether an empty tool result (a real occurrence) should be a rejection is a product call.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/validate.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.2\",\"AC-4.4\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-2\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"CLI parses flags with strict:false, silently ignoring unknown or misspelled flags\",\"evidence\":\"src/cli/index.ts:53-64 calls parseArgs with strict: false, so `lhc threads new-thread --file-path p --titel x` creates the thread with no title and no error. This is the silent-drop failure mode the epic's boundary philosophy ('Nothing is silently dropped') exists to prevent, though the closed-contract rule formally covers the event envelope, not argv. A strict: true parse with a structured usage error would align the CLI boundary with the SDK boundary's strictness.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-002\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Read-back surfaces skip thread-reference validation at both SDK and CLI layers\",\"evidence\":\"Merged from epic-reviewer-1 NB-3 and epic-reviewer-2 F-EPIC-002 — the same gap at two layers. SDK: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92, 267), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations pass the reference straight to resolveThreadRef, so unknown fields or an empty filePath are tolerated. CLI: `messages list`, `messages list-queued-work`, `turns list`, and `turns list-queued-work` (cli/index.ts:176-183) lack the --thread-id/--file-path missing-flag guard the intake commands have, and threadRefFrom defaults a missing path to '' (cli/index.ts:79); verified this pass that `node dist/cli.js messages list` returns thread_not_found for an empty path instead of a usage error. Behavior remains fail-closed; the inconsistency is in which error the caller gets and whether unknown ref fields are named.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.6\",\"AC-4.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-4\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Mutable test seams exported from the production pipeline module\",\"evidence\":\"setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:23, 34) are module-global mutable state in production code, reachable by any importer of the internal module path even though the boundary check confines them to test/fixtures and the package export map exposes only the SDK root. Unset they are inert (real wall clock when unset; explicit clock argument wins). The walk hook is design-sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row; the clock setter is an addition beyond the design's context-injected clock. Consider relocating behind a single test-only entry or documenting them in the design's seam inventory when Epic 02 extends the context.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/intake-seam.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-5\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Dead stub helper and stale dist artifacts left from earlier phases\",\"evidence\":\"shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no callers since the Story 0 CLI stubs were replaced (verified: only the definition remains in src/). dist/ retains compiled output from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/ confirmed present) because tsc does not clean; the package is private so nothing ships, but a clean step would prevent confusion about what the production build contains.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts\",\"packages/lhc/dist\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[\"Whether epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule applies to payload text/content fields (NB-1): the epic prose says reject, the tech design's typed interfaces say accept, and the implementation follows the design. Needs a human ruling and an erratum on whichever document loses.\",\"The work_item table has no UNIQUE/status constraints beyond the primary key by design (Epic 02 adds lifecycle); confirm Epic 02's schema migration owns adding the status vocabulary constraint so 'queued'-only is not silently relied on.\"],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"},\"verifierResults\":[{\"resultId\":\"121ad125-b9d9-4f3e-b3d0-1ea2dc538681\",\"outcome\":\"pass\",\"provider\":\"claude-code\",\"model\":\"claude-fable-5\",\"reviewerLabel\":\"epic-reviewer-1\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"The five stories compose into one coherent pipeline exactly as designed: validate (pure, pre-lock) → BEGIN IMMEDIATE → corruption check → per-event [dedup → record → turn transition → projection with membership stamp → work queueing] → walk-time result assembly → COMMIT (src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts:84-254). The transition-before-stamp ordering is implemented and TC-3.8's two-turns-in-one-batch case works without lookahead.\",\"Deterministic id scheme is consistent across stories: m<eventOrder> (messages/index.ts:63), t<turnOrder> (turns/internal/store.ts:27), w-<sourceId>-<kind> (tech-utils/work-queue/index.ts:40), matching tech-design decision 7; work-item ids are naturally idempotent for the Epic 02 repair path.\",\"The error model (OpResult, three classes, stable codes) is uniform across all four domain surfaces and the CLI; each class has exactly one producer (validation → caller_error, corruption check → state_corruption, wrapped SQLite/fs → system_error) as the design requires, and TC-4.4/turns tests assert all three classes against each other through both SDK and CLI.\",\"Later stories' tests build their substrate through earlier stories' real surfaces (threadWith-style helpers create threads via newThread and record via messageEvents), giving the continuous upstream re-verification the design called for; no story mocks a neighbor.\",\"Minor inconsistency: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92-93, 267-268), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations skip validateThreadRef entirely — an unknown field on a read-back thread reference is silently tolerated there. The epic's closed-contract rule formally binds only the intake envelope, so this is drift in rigor, not a contract violation.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"Module structure matches the tech design's layout exactly (domains/{threads,intake-stream,messages,turns} with index.ts + internal/, tech-utils/{work-queue,token-counting}, shared/{context,errors,storage}, cli/{index,render}, sdk.ts); the boundary check (scripts/check-boundaries.mjs) enforces both rules with the fixtures exemption and passes over 38 files.\",\"Import boundaries hold: all cross-domain calls go through domain index surfaces; the only below-SDK reach is test/fixtures/ (corrupt.ts, intake-seam.ts, legacy.ts), which is the design's sanctioned exemption.\",\"Beyond-design addition (sound): the thread-file schema is a versioned migration history (v1 events, v2 messages, v3 turns, v4 work items) with lazy upgrade through openThreadDatabase and identity validation before any migration (threads/internal/create.ts:28-190). This is not in the tech design but is consistent with file-is-authority, is well-tested (F-03-001/F-03-002, thread-migration.test.ts), and protects pre-story files.\",\"Interface drift (benign): messages.createFromEvent takes a third turnId parameter (messages/index.ts:55-59) versus the design's two-arg signature — the natural realization of the design's own stamp-after-transition rule; turns.applyEvent matches the design signature exactly.\",\"Contract drift against the epic: Flow 4 declares 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' invalid, but validate.ts accepts empty-string text (user_prompt/assistant_text/assistant_thinking/runtime_note) and empty tool_result.content (validate.ts:55-65 uses Schema.String, not NonEmptyString), while rejecting empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey. The tech design's interfaces typed these as plain string, so the drift originates between epic and design. See non-blocking finding NB-1.\",\"Transaction boundaries, BEGIN IMMEDIATE, file-then-row creation with compensation, registry lazy-init with non-creating reads, tokenizer pinning (js-tiktoken:o200k_base recorded in thread metadata at creation), and the operation-context shape all match the design decisions 2, 3, 6, and 8 as written.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"All 33 TCs from the epic appear as greppably-tagged tests and pass: Flow 1 (TC-1.1–1.6 with CLI legs), Flow 2 (TC-2.1–2.9), Flow 3 (TC-3.1–3.8 plus the golden state-machine suite covering every rule-table row), Flow 4 (TC-4.1–4.5 plus the unknown-field and empty actor/harness supplementals), Flow 5 (TC-5.1–5.5 with the below-SDK rawDump absence scan for TC-5.5).\",\"Both named cross-chunk deferrals are explicitly closed in their owing stories: TC-1.4 id/path equivalence lives in intake.test.ts titled 'closes Story 1's deferral', and the TC-3.3/TC-3.6 work halves are paid in work-queue.test.ts titled 'Story 4's debt paid'.\",\"All eight architecture-risk rows from the test plan exist and pass: mid-walk rollback via the induced-failure seam, restart survival extended to work items, rejected-batch-takes-no-lock, system_error rollback parity, fixture validity, boundary-check coverage, registry lazy-init non-creation, plus migration coverage beyond the plan.\",\"Test quality is substantive, not title-deep: durability asserted through raw SQL row scans (work-queue.test.ts:64-72, 300-310), rejection asserted through full five-surface logical read-back diffs against baselines, and the 300KB tool-result round-trip runs through both SDK and spawned-binary stdin.\",\"111 tests across 16 files versus the plan's ~72 estimate — the overage is migration tests, CLI-process splits per story, and supplemental strictness cases, all in-scope.\",\"Gates: verify-all (epic gate) passes end to end including the spawned-binary CLI process suite; green-verify (story gate) passes including the Red-manifest test-immutability check (33+ red-phase files unchanged).\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No fake adapters, mocks, or stubbed success exist anywhere on the production path: storage is real node:sqlite (WAL, foreign keys, busy_timeout — shared/storage.ts:8-15), token counting is real js-tiktoken o200k_base, and every CLI command body is one SDK call plus JSON rendering. The test suite's what-gets-mocked-nothing claim holds in the code.\",\"Two module-level test seams live in the production pipeline module: setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:21-36). Both are null/no-op in production, settable only through the boundary-exempt fixtures directory, and fail safe (real wall clock when unset; explicit clock argument wins). The walk hook is design-sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row; the clock setter is an implementation addition beyond the design's context-injected clock. Reported as material per the audit mandate; neither fakes success or alters production behavior.\",\"Dead code: shared/errors.ts:36-45 notImplemented() is the Story 0 fail-closed stub vocabulary with no remaining callers.\",\"Stale build artifacts in dist/ from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) are not produced by the current src/ and would ship under files:[\\\"dist\\\"] if the package were ever published (it is private). The bin target dist/cli.js is current and rebuilt by every gate run.\",\"newThread stamps createdAt from the raw wall clock with no injection seam (threads/index.ts:85), a small departure from the test plan's claim that the injected clock covers createdAt; no test needed it and production behavior is correct.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"NB-1\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Empty-string payload text/content accepted despite epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule\",\"evidence\":\"Epic Flow 4 lists 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' as invalid. validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63), so { text: \\\"\\\" } and empty tool_result content pass validation, while empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey are rejected via NonEmptyString. The tech design's interface section typed these as plain string, so design and epic disagree; the implementation followed the design. Whether an empty tool result (a real occurrence — tools legitimately return empty output) should be a rejection is a product call.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/validate.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.2\",\"AC-4.4\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"human-ruling\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-2\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"CLI parses flags with strict:false, silently ignoring unknown or misspelled flags\",\"evidence\":\"cli/index.ts:53-64 calls parseArgs with strict: false, so `lhc threads new-thread --file-path p --titel x` creates the thread with no title and no error. This is the silent-drop failure mode the epic's boundary philosophy ('Nothing is silently dropped') exists to prevent, though the closed-contract rule formally covers the event envelope, not argv. A strict: true parse with a structured usage error would align the CLI boundary with the SDK boundary's strictness.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-4.7\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-3\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Read-back surfaces skip closed thread-reference validation\",\"evidence\":\"messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92, 267), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations pass the reference straight to resolveThreadRef, so an unknown field or empty filePath on a read-back call is tolerated (empty path surfaces as thread_not_found rather than invalid_event). Behavior is still fail-closed; the inconsistency is in which error the caller gets and whether unknown ref fields are named.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-4\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Mutable test seams exported from the production pipeline module\",\"evidence\":\"setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:21-36) are module-global mutable state in production code, reachable by any importer of the internal module path even though the in-repo boundary check confines them to test/fixtures and the package export map exposes only the SDK root. Unset they are inert. Consider relocating behind a single test-only entry or documenting them in the design's seam inventory when Epic 02 extends the context.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/intake-seam.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false},{\"id\":\"NB-5\",\"severity\":\"observation\",\"title\":\"Dead stub helper and stale dist artifacts left from earlier phases\",\"evidence\":\"shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no callers since the Story 0 CLI stubs were replaced. dist/ retains compiled output from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/) because tsc does not clean; the package is private so nothing ships, but a clean step or removing the strays would prevent confusion about what the production build contains.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts\",\"packages/lhc/dist\"],\"requirementIds\":[],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[\"Whether epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule applies to payload text/content fields (NB-1): the epic prose says reject, the tech design's typed interfaces say accept, and the implementation follows the design. Needs a human ruling and an erratum on whichever document loses.\",\"The work_item table has no UNIQUE/status constraints beyond the primary key by design (Epic 02 adds lifecycle); confirm Epic 02's schema migration owns adding the status vocabulary constraint so 'queued'-only is not silently relied on.\"],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"},{\"resultId\":\"a5f0f865-007a-4fe3-9cb4-04c3b2214706\",\"outcome\":\"block\",\"provider\":\"codex\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.5\",\"reviewerLabel\":\"epic-reviewer-2\",\"crossStoryFindings\":[\"Stories 1-5 are wired through the same SDK/CLI production path and both required gates pass, but Story 1's thread-creation contract has an untested edge that breaks every later story by registering a thread with no durable file.\"],\"architectureFindings\":[\"The domain/import structure matches the tech-design layout and the boundary gate passed: `boundaries: OK (38 files checked, fixtures exempt)`.\",\"Thread creation does not validate non-empty paths at the SDK boundary; `newThread({ filePath: \\\"\\\" })` reaches `node:sqlite`, which opens a temporary database, then inserts a registry row for `filePath: \\\"\\\"`.\"],\"epicCoverageAssessment\":[\"The implemented tests cover the epic TC table broadly: `verify-all` reported 16 files and 111 tests passing.\",\"Coverage is missing for invalid empty thread file paths on `threads.newThread` and the CLI `threads new-thread --file-path ''` path, so AC-1.1/AC-1.3/AC-1.4 are not fully proven.\"],\"productionPathFindings\":[\"No material fake adapter, shim, or placeholder execution path was found in the accepted intake, projection, turn, or work-queue flow; the stale `notImplemented` helper is present but unused by production commands.\",\"Material production-path defect: the real SDK and real built CLI can create a registry row for an empty path without creating a thread file. Exact observed CLI sequence: `threads new-thread --file-path ''` returned `{ ok: true, value: { threadId: \\\"th_0fea4c8735507259\\\", filePath: \\\"\\\" } }`; `threads resolve` returned that row; `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_0fea4c8735507259` then returned `{ ok: false, error: { errorClass: \\\"caller_error\\\", code: \\\"thread_not_found\\\", reason: \\\"no thread file exists at \\\" } }`.\"],\"blockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-001\",\"severity\":\"major\",\"title\":\"Thread creation accepts an empty path and registers a non-existent thread file\",\"evidence\":\"`packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts:73` only checks `existsSync(input.filePath)` before `createThreadFile`. Node's `DatabaseSync(\\\"\\\")` succeeds as a temporary database, so `newThread({ filePath: \\\"\\\" })` returned `ok: true` with `filePath: \\\"\\\"`, no file existed at that path, and the registry still resolved the generated thread id to `\\\"\\\"`. The built CLI reproduced the same behavior with `threads new-thread --file-path ''`, after which `intake-stream list-events --thread-id <id>` failed with `thread_not_found`.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"AC-1.1\",\"AC-1.3\",\"AC-1.4\",\"TC-1.1\",\"TC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":true}],\"nonBlockingFindings\":[{\"id\":\"F-EPIC-002\",\"severity\":\"minor\",\"title\":\"Some read-back CLI commands do not enforce the required thread reference flags\",\"evidence\":\"`packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts:176` through `packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts:183` call `messages.list`, `messages.list-queued-work`, `turns.list`, and `turns list-queued-work` without the missing `--thread-id`/`--file-path` guard used by intake commands. Running `node packages/lhc/dist/cli.js messages list` returned `thread_not_found` for an empty path instead of a usage/missing-flag error.\",\"affectedFiles\":[\"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/projection.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/turns.test.ts\",\"packages/lhc/test/work-queue.test.ts\"],\"requirementIds\":[\"CLI conventions\",\"AC-1.6\"],\"recommendedFixScope\":\"quick-fix\",\"blocking\":false}],\"unresolvedItems\":[],\"gateResult\":\"pass\"}]},\"errors\":[],\"warnings\":[],\"artifacts\":[{\"kind\":\"result-envelope\",\"path\":\"/Users/leemoore/code/pi-long-horizon/liminal-context/packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/01-thread-record-and-intake/artifacts/epic/001-epic-review.json\"}],\"startedAt\":\"2026-06-10T15:12:46.467Z\",\"finishedAt\":\"2026-06-10T15:25:39.208Z\"}\n","numLines":2,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2}}}
